# Upgrade-path probe — can a profile that installed an OLD version be upgraded, and by which command
#
#   node deepblend/tools/upgrade-path-probe.mjs | tee deepblend/docs/probe-upgrade-path.log
#
# Taken 2026-09-23 (commercial-readiness ledger C3). What it is evidence for: `install.md` §6's upgrade
# answer, which did not exist before this run because nobody had asked the question with a command.
#
# WHY IT IS WORTH A PROBE. Three routes install, and (since C2) they are compared and agree. What a
# user asks SECOND is "I have last month's build — how do I get this month's?", and the sharp edge is
# the tarball route: its asset name carries no version (the market's `/releases/latest/download/`
# rule), so the URL is byte-identical between releases while pnpm's store is keyed by that URL. A
# machine that installed the previous release and asks for the same URL gets the previous artifact —
# measured in `milestone-status.md` §199.6, where it looked exactly like a failed release.
#
# HOW IT IS MEASURED. Both old specs are real and still addressable: `@deepblend/dsh-blender-bundle@0.2.1`
# on the registry, and `…/releases/download/v0.2.1/deepblend-bundle.tgz` — a Release asset answers to
# its TAG even though the version-free name only ever serves the latest, which is what makes this
# question answerable at all. Each route gets its own scratch `$DSH_HOME` and its own EMPTY pnpm store,
# so no cache can answer for the network.
#
# THE ANSWER, AND THE MECHANISM. The two routes behave differently, and the `recorded spec` lines say
# why: the ecosystem's `add` leaves an EXISTING dependency's spec alone.
#
#   npm      0.2.1 → re-add the bare name → still 0.2.1 (spec unchanged: 0.2.1)
#                   → remove + add       → 0.2.2  (spec becomes ^0.2.2)
#   tarball  0.2.1 → re-add the current URL → 0.2.2 (the spec CHANGED: v0.2.1 → latest)
#
# So: **re-running `add` upgrades the tarball route and does NOT upgrade the npm route.** The command
# that works on both is `dsh plugin remove` then `dsh plugin add`, which is now what `install.md` §6
# tells a reader — with the reason, because "run these two" without "why" is the shape of instruction
# this repository keeps replacing with a command.
#
# WHAT IS NOT MEASURED HERE: the SOURCE route. It tracks the default branch, so it has no old version
# to start from — its spec string is the same before and after, which by the npm route's mechanism
# means re-adding it would not move either. That is an inference from the measurement above, not a
# reading, and it is written down as one.

